## Gates

**Never pipe a gate command; run it as its own command and read its exit code directly.**
Why: a pipeline takes the exit code of its last command, so a failing gate reports clean.

**Redirect deploy CLI output to a file instead of piping it, and verify the deploy independently.**
Why: a closed pipe can kill an upload mid-deploy and leave the old code live.

**When `{{BUILD_CMD}}` already runs the typecheck, it is the whole build gate; keep `{{TYPECHECK_CMD}}` for quick signal mid-iteration.**
Why: a second typecheck in the gate is a repeat, not a second check.

**Run the full suite (`TZ={{LOCAL_TZ}} {{TEST_CMD}}`) in the foreground as its own command, never backgrounded behind a polling loop.**
Why: a foreground run yields the exit code directly, while polling adds no information and leaves it unclear whether the gate settled.

**Before every test launch, scoped or full, check for leftover test-runner processes started from this repo: `root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" && pgrep -fl "$(printf '%s' "$root" | sed 's/[][\.*^$+?(){}|]/\\&/g')/node_modules/.*{{TEST_RUNNER_PROC}}"`. It lists only processes run from this repo's own `node_modules`, and exits 1 when there are none.**
Why: a run stacked on a previous run's live workers contends for the same cores and looks exactly like a hang.

**Wait for or stop only a process that check lists; never stop or wait on one it does not.**
Why: a match by runner name alone also catches another project's run, which is not this session's to stop or wait on; this is the parallel-session rule under Session Workflow, applied to test runs.

**Session order: implement → scoped runs (`{{SCOPED_TEST_CMD}}`) → break probes observed failing on scoped runs → restore by copy and confirm with `diff` → the related run (`{{RELATED_TEST_CMD}}`) and a grep for every changed symbol → spec-update protocol → scoped doc contracts → one `{{BUILD_CMD}}` → one full suite on the final bytes.**
Why: the single full run on the committed bytes proves everything an earlier full run would, at a fraction of the cost.

**Before the gate, run the tests that import the changed files: `{{RELATED_TEST_CMD}}`, with the files `git diff --name-only HEAD` and `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` list, in the foreground and unpiped. Then, for every symbol the change renamed, removed or changed the meaning of, run `command grep -rnw --exclude-dir=node_modules --exclude-dir=.git <symbol> .` and check each hit. A related run that reports no test files found has tested nothing; say so in the report. It is a scoped run, not a full one; after a fix, rerun it, not the suite.**
Why: a scoped run of the touched file misses the tests that import it, and the related run follows only static imports, so a renamed symbol can survive in a dynamically loaded module, a script or a doc that no import reaches.

**The full suite runs once per session, as the last gate on the final bytes. Banned: a baseline run on the untouched tree, a run before the break probes, a run before a push that is not the final gate, and a full run under `{{SECOND_TZ}}`. If the one run fails on a test the change does not touch, run that test's file alone once; if it passes alone, report it as load-sensitive with both timings and run the full suite one more time, at most, and never raise a timeout to get past it.**
Why: every full run before the last proves nothing the last one does not, and a raised timeout hides a real slowdown.

**Break probes: one per guard the prompt names, plus any guard the session judges would fail silently, each observed failing on a scoped run of the touched file. A probe that changes no behavior is reported as meaningless, not counted, and more than eight in a session needs a one-line reason in the report.**
Why: a probe count is not evidence; a named guard seen failing is.

**Restore a break probe from a copy made before the break, confirm the restore with `diff`, and never restore with `git checkout`.**
Why: checkout discards every uncommitted change in the file, not just the break, and an unconfirmed restore may be incomplete.

**A change touching any non-Markdown file, scripts included, owes the full suite; only an all-Markdown change runs just the doc contracts.**
Why: a script nobody deploys still runs through the same tests, and a docs-only gate on a code change is how a red test reaches main.

**A probe or new test proves nothing until it has been seen to change behavior, failing against a deliberate break.**
Why: a check that passes against its own poison is a statement about the check, not the code.

**In a proof or leak check, call grep as `command grep`, and record `type grep` with the proof.**
Why: a session shell's `grep` can be a wrapper that honors `.gitignore`: a recursive search through it skips every ignored file, such as `.claude/project-instructions.local.md`, while still reading untracked files and any file named outright, so a leak check passes over exactly the files it exists to cover.

**Run the suite locally under one timezone, `{{LOCAL_TZ}}`; when a change touches date, timezone or calendar-day logic, also run the target files, not the suite, under `{{SECOND_TZ}}`.**
Why: the second leg is cheap for a handful of files and is the only per-change coverage of the other side of midnight.

**Find timezone-sensitive tests by re-running the recorded grep (`{{TZ_TEST_GREP}}`), never by trusting a saved list.**
Why: a list rots, and the grep over-matches on purpose, so its hits are a list to inspect, not a verdict.

**Prefer tests that pin their own TZ and clock, run both directions and assert the results diverged.**
Why: they stop depending on the ambient runner's timezone.

**Anchor fixture dates to the clock with a literal offset (an order created "now minus 45 days"), never a literal date that a computed cutoff compares against.**
Why: a literal date ages out of its window months later and fails on an unrelated commit; a literal offset, unlike one derived from the constant under test, keeps the test independent.

**Write fixture dates as `daysAgo(n)` / `dateDaysAgo(n)` with a literal `n`; a test that injects a pinned clock into the code under test anchors both ends or neither; the ratchet in the dev-tools `ratchet-tests` skill (`skills/ratchet-tests`) fails the diff that adds a literal date.**
Why: fixtures anchored to the real clock march forward while an injected cutoff stays put, which builds a new expiry date into the fix for the old one.

<!-- BEGIN:scheduled-ci -->
**The local gate is the primary gate; scheduled CI is a backstop that catches a skipped local gate up to a day late, so check the latest scheduled run at session pre-flight; the session-start check (`sh scripts/session-start.sh`) prints it with `{{SCHEDULED_CI_CMD}}`.**
Why: a red scheduled run means something already slipped past a local gate.
<!-- END:scheduled-ci -->

