## Supabase

Depth on RLS, column grants, views and definer functions lives in the dev-tools `supabase-hardening` skill. Only the always-on rules are here.

<!-- BEGIN:schema-by-hand -->
**Apply schema changes by hand in the dashboard SQL editor; `supabase db push` is denied at the permission layer (`.claude/settings.json`).**
Why: the CLI's migration history conflicts with changes applied by hand, and the push fails; the deny rule is committed so it travels with every clone.

**Every paste file opens with a guard that checks the live state it was written against (each object it creates is absent, each one it alters matches what was read, a function by `md5(prosrc)`) and raises if any differs, so the paste fails whole instead of applying.**
Why: a paste written against yesterday's schema applies cleanly to today's and does something else, and the rolled-back proof vouched only for the state it ran on.
<!-- END:schema-by-hand -->
<!-- BEGIN:schema-by-claude -->
**Claude Code applies schema changes only with the writable connector's `apply_migration`, one migration per change, and an ask rule in `.claude/settings.json` puts each call in front of the user; `supabase db push` is denied at the permission layer.**
Why: the CLI keeps its own migration history, which conflicts with changes applied any other way, and the push fails; both rules are committed so they travel with every clone, and the ask holds in auto mode too.
<!-- END:schema-by-claude -->
<!-- BEGIN:migration-deny -->

**The writable connector's `apply_migration` is denied in `.claude/settings.json` too, keyed to the name the connector was added under in Claude Code; after re-adding any connector, re-check the rules with `/permissions` in a terminal `claude` session.**
Why: Claude.ai's tool toggles never reach Claude Code, and Claude.ai connectors appear here under ID prefixes, so a rule keyed to one silently stops matching when it is re-added, and a deny that stops matching fails open.
<!-- END:migration-deny -->

**Edge Functions do not deploy on git push: deploy in the same session as the edit, before the commit, and verify the deployment identifier (entrypoint) and update time advanced.**
Why: otherwise the repo and the live function silently disagree, and the version counter alone can move without a deploy.

```bash
supabase functions deploy <name> --project-ref {{SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF}} > deploy.log 2>&1
```
<!-- BEGIN:supabase-cli -->

**The Supabase CLI's credential is the `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN` the committed SessionStart hook in `.claude/settings.json` loads at session start, for this project only, from `~/.config/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/`, which sessions never read or write; run CLI commands plain (`supabase ...`), never through `env`, an inline assignment or a wrapper, never run `supabase login`, and run the session-start check (`sh scripts/session-start.sh`) at pre-flight. To confirm a documentation line that names the variable, or to write a commit message that does, put the text in a file and use `command grep -cxF -f <file>` or `git commit -F <file>`, for those two purposes only.**
Why: committed rules match the command text from its start, so a wrapped form is a different string of the same action, a login stores an account-wide token the CLI falls back to, and a session that needs a different credential has found a setup bug, not a reason to prefix.
<!-- END:supabase-cli -->

**Run `REVOKE ALL ... FROM PUBLIC, anon, authenticated` before any targeted GRANT on a new function or table, and again after every DROP + CREATE; verify with the raw ACL.**
Why: default privileges grant EXECUTE and table access to those roles at creation, and DROP + CREATE silently resets an ACL that CREATE OR REPLACE would have kept.

**Use the read-only MCP connector for verification; reserve the writable connector for rolled-back probes and deploys.**
Why: a read that cannot write cannot damage production, and the writable connector's extra reach is needed only where a transaction must run.

**The writable connector is scoped to this project (its URL carries `project_ref=`) and added in Claude Code at local scope (`claude mcp add --transport http <name> --scope local <url>`); never use an account-wide writable connector, and never commit an MCP config (`.mcp.json` or any other) naming a Supabase server.**
Why: an account-wide connector takes the project ID as an argument, so one wrong ID changes another project's database, and a committed `.mcp.json` hands a writable server to every session that opens the repo, in every clone; this is the parallel-session rule under Session Workflow, applied to databases.

**Use only the connectors scoped to this project's ref; connectors added in Claude.ai load in every Claude Code session, so another project's may be listed too.**
Why: another project's connector answers from its own database, and a result from it can pass for this project's.
<!-- BEGIN:connectors -->

**This project's connectors are `{{SUPABASE_RO_CONNECTOR}}` (read-only, in Claude.ai) and `{{SUPABASE_RW_CONNECTOR}}` (writable, in Claude Code at local scope); never use any other.**
Why: other projects' connectors appear in every chat and every Claude Code session on the account, so a session sees them all, and only these two answer from this project's database.
<!-- END:connectors -->
<!-- END:supabase -->

