## Running in auto mode

In auto mode a classifier, not the user, approves most actions. It reads this file, so the facts below are written for it as much as for the session.

**`.claude/settings.json`'s deny rules block in every mode, and its ask rules force a prompt in every mode, auto included; a headless run (`claude -p`) has no prompt to show, so it refuses an ask-gated action.**
Why: those rules are evaluated before the classifier, so they are the only limits that hold whoever approves; a limit stated only in a prompt or a chat is something the classifier weighs, and it can be lost when the context is compacted.

**Never remove or loosen a rule in `.claude/settings.json`; the doc-contract test fails when one the setup wrote goes missing.**
Why: in auto mode an edit under `.claude/` is judged by the classifier instead of being shown to the user, so a deleted rule would go unseen until the action it gated ran.

Facts:
